<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teatro di San Carlo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teatro di San Carlo is the opera house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, opened on 4 November 1737 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> next to the royal palace. It is the oldest opera house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> still in continuous use and was a leading centre of Italian opera, with works by Rossini and Donizetti premiered there. After a fire in 1816 it was rebuilt within a year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo.jpg" alt="Teatro di San Carlo" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_de_San_Carlos,_N%C3%A1poles,_Italia,_2023-03-25,_DD_184-186_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Teatro di San Carlo stands next to the Royal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Piazza del Plebiscito. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king Charles VII of Naples commissioned it to replace the old, dilapidated and too small Teatro San Bartolomeo of 1621. It was designed by Giovanni Antonio de Medrano, a Sicilian nobleman and royal architect, with Angelo Carasale, the former director of the San Bartolomeo, responsible for the furnishings. The horseshoe-shaped auditorium was built for 75,000 ducats and had 184 boxes in six orders and a royal box for ten people. Blue and gold, the Bourbon colours, dominated the decoration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was inaugurated on 4 November 1737, the king's name day, with Domenico Sarro's opera Achille in Sciro, to a libretto by Metastasio. The first seasons showed the royal preference for dance and featured castrati. Gluck was called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> to direct his Clemenza di Tito in 1752. Domenico Barbaia became manager of the royal opera houses in 1809. On 13 February 1816 a fire broke out during a dress rehearsal for a ballet and destroyed part of the building. On the orders of King Ferdinand IV, Barbaia rebuilt it within ten months with the architect Antonio Niccolini, and it reopened on 12 January 1817 with Johann Simon Mayr's Il sogno di Partenope.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gioachino-rossini">Gioachino Rossini</a> was house composer and artistic director of the royal opera houses from 1815 to 1822 and wrote ten operas in this period, among them Otello and Mosè in Egitto. Gaetano Donizetti followed from 1822 until 1838 and composed sixteen operas for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, including Lucia di Lammermoor in 1835. Verdi wrote Alzira and Luisa Miller for it. After the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1861 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> lost its status as the musical centre of Italy, and by 1874 falling income closed the house for a year. Bombs damaged it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and its first opera performance after the liberation of Naples in 1943 was La bohème on 26 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The San Carlo is the oldest continuously active opera venue in the world, opened decades before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>'s La Scala or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s La Fenice. Its opera season runs from late November to July, and its ballet season from December to early June. The seating, once 3,285, has been reduced to 1,386. Since the 1844 redecoration under Niccolini the interior has been red and gold. A renovation costing 67 million euros, carried out in 2008 and 2009, was meant to return the house to its condition after the rebuilding of 1816, and it reopened on 27 January 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teatro di San Carlo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles of Bourbon founded the <b>Teatro di San Carlo</b> in 1737, began the palace of Caserta and sponsored the excavations of Herculaneum and Pompeii, which shaped European Neoclassicism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Real <b>Teatro di San Carlo</b>, opened on 4 November 1737 as the biggest opera house in the world, was rebuilt within ten months of the fire of 13 February 1816 by Antonio Niccolini as a horseshoe of 1,444 seats.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
